<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Listen! – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's a School Day!</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Listen!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Identify objects on the pages of picture books, using simple words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can identify objects in picture books by pointing and using single words (e.g., pointing to a picture of a chair and saying 'chair').<br>Can name some objects on the pages of picture books during choral reading activities (e.g., pointing to and naming objects like 'pen' or 'book' on the pages of a picture book during choral reading activities).<br>Can point to objects in response to a prompt by the teacher during choral reading (e.g., pointing to objects, such as a 'window' or 'door' on a picture book page in response to the prompt, 'Where is it?' during choral reading).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Listen and Do" activity using known classroom commands and objects. Teacher says, "Please, stand up!" (pronouncing /s/ in 'stand up'), "Touch your desk!", "Open your book!" (initial sound /b/). Students perform the actions. The teacher asks, "Are you ready to listen?" (pronunciation /l/ in 'listen').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models explaining simple rules and identifying objects. For example, the teacher points to a "Classroom Rules Poster" (partially prepared or an example) showing a child listening and says, "This child is listening. We say: 'Listen, please!'" (mediating the visual to verbal). The teacher points to a desk and says, "It is a desk." Teacher clearly pronounces 'teacher' and 'student' with correct syllable stress.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a child standing and asks, "Are they sitting or standing?" (Students respond: "Standing!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a book and asks, "Is this a window?" (Students respond: "No!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful picture book titled "My School Day Rules" (narrative excerpt with illustrations of a child's day at school, including classroom objects and visual rules). The book features visuals of a chair, desk, table, child, book, window, door, teacher, student, and actions like listen, stand up, jump, touch, open, emphasizing "It is a school" and polite imperatives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying objects in picture books and understanding simple commands, using basic words to give an instruction and respond to WH-questions like "What is it?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> Teacher shows the cover and asks, "Is this a house or a school?" (Students respond chorally: "A school!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Point and Say":</b> As the teacher reads, she points to an object in the book (e.g., a window) and asks, "What is it?" Students respond, "A window!" The teacher also asks, "Where is the door?" (Students point in the book).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Action Identification":</b> Teacher points to a picture of a child listening and asks, "What is the child doing?" Students reply, "Listen!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Mediation (explaining visuals with words, identifying objects); Grammar: Imperatives (Stand up, listen), Present simple (It is a...), WH-questions (What is it?, Where is it?); Vocabulary: chair, desk, table, book, window, door, teacher, student, listen, stand up, touch, open.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - "Picture-Command Match":</b> The teacher distributes small cards with pictures of classroom objects (chair, desk, book, door) and action visuals (child standing, child listening). The teacher then says a command (e.g., "Stand up, please!") or an identification ("It is a book!"). Students must hold up the corresponding picture card. This mediates the verbal command/identification to the visual.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - "Show and Tell Rule":</b> Students work in small groups with large flashcards depicting basic classroom rules (e.g., "Listen, please!", "Sit down, please!", "Open your book!"). One student shows a card, and the other students say the corresponding command or identify the object/action in the picture. The teacher circulates, asking "What is it?" or "What do we say?".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Classroom Object Scavenger Hunt":</b> Students work in pairs. One student says, "Find the window!" (or "Where is the window?"), and the partner points to the actual window in the classroom or a picture of it. The partner then says, "Window!" or "It is a window!" They take turns. This reinforces identifying and mediating realia/pictures to words.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> "Classroom Rules Poster Explanation." Students will present and explain their collaborative "Classroom Rules Poster," mediating the visual rules into simple verbal commands and identifications.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Small group presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in their assigned groups, each with their pre-made "Classroom Rules Poster" (featuring simple drawings/pictures for rules like 'Listen, please!', 'Stand up, please!', 'Sit down, please!').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group takes turns presenting their poster to the class.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">As they present, the teacher or another student points to a visual rule on their poster (e.g., a picture of a child sitting calmly).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The group members collectively or individually mediate the visual by stating the rule using an imperative: "Sit down, please!" or "Be calm!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher will also point to objects drawn on the poster (e.g., a chair, a book) and ask, "What is it?" Students respond, "It is a chair." or "It is a book." (Initial sound /t/ in 'touch' if rule involves touching, /s/ in 'school' if related to school environment, /l/ in 'listen', /b/ in 'book').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on their ability to explain the meaning of the visuals and identify related objects using the target language.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Classroom Rules Poster Explanation."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Classroom Rules Poster" and respond to teacher prompts about the visuals and rules, demonstrating their ability to mediate the visual information into simple English words and commands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., /s/ in 'school', /l/ in 'listen', syllable stress in 'teacher', 'student').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary to identify objects on the poster (e.g., 'chair', 'desk', 'book').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses imperatives to state rules from the poster (e.g., 'Stand up, please!', 'Listen, please!').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to "What is it?" by identifying objects (e.g., "It is a door.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Points to objects in response to "Where is it?" (e.g., pointing to the 'window' on the poster).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates with peers during the group presentation.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students receive a simple paper with two sentences and a space for a happy or sad face.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can point to a picture of a [picture of a book] and say 'book'." (Draw a happy or sad face).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can tell my friends 'Stand up, please!' when they see the picture." (Draw a happy or sad face).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompts:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What rule on our poster is easy to remember and say?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is your favorite thing in the classroom that we learned to say in English?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students mediate visual rules and objects into simple verbal English? (e.g., TPR, visual aids, repetitive questioning).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks with young learners? (e.g., more one-on-one prompts, simpler visuals, longer choral practice). Did the pace allow all students to participate adequately?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This is the final lesson for "Let's Listen!". The learning from this theme, especially identifying objects and following/giving simple commands, will provide a strong foundation for new vocabulary and scenarios in the next thematic unit, which will continue to build on their ability to mediate information in different contexts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>